Here's a sample implementation of this algorithm in C++:
```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n)
{
    // Find the maximum element in the array
    int max = INT_MIN;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        if (arr[i] > max)
            max = arr[i];

    // Scan through the remaining elements to find the second largest element
    int secondMax = -1;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (arr[i] != max && (secondMax == -1 || arr[i] > secondMax))
            secondMax = arr[i];
    }

    return secondMax;
}
```
The `findSecondLargest` function takes an array of integers (`arr[]`) and its length (`n`) as inputs, and returns the second largest integer element in the array. It uses two nested loops to iterate over the array: the outer loop selects the maximum element, and the inner loop finds the second largest element. 
This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n), since both loops run in linear time relative to the size of the input array. It also handles all possible cases, including arrays with duplicate values or empty arrays.

For finding the second largest integer element in an unsorted array, one common algorithm is the selection sort algorithm. This algorithm works by repeatedly selecting the smallest (or largest) element from the unsorted portion of the array and moving it to the end of the sorted portion until the entire array is sorted.
Here's a C++ function that implements this algorithm:
```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n) {
    // Sort the array using selection sort algorithm
    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        int minIndex = i;
        for (int j = i+1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < arr[minIndex]) {
                minIndex = j;
            }
        }
        swap(arr[i], arr[minIndex]);
    }

    // Return the second largest element
    return arr[n-2];
}
int main() {
    int arr[] = {45, 32, 89, 67, 23};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

    cout << "The second largest element is: " << findSecondLargest(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```
This function first sorts the array using the selection sort algorithm. During each iteration of the outer loop, it finds the index of the minimum element in the unsorted portion of the array. It then swaps this element with the current element at the start of the array. After sorting the entire array, the second largest element will be at index `n-2`. The function returns this value.
To ensure optimal runtime efficiency, the selection sort algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2), which is not very efficient for large arrays. However, it provides a simple implementation that doesn't require any additional data structures or algorithms. Additionally, the function checks for edge cases where the array might have fewer than two elements and returns -1 in such cases.
Overall, the selection sort algorithm is a good starting point for finding the second largest element in an unsorted array, but there are other algorithms available with better time complexities, like quicksort, mergesort, etc.

